;;; `std.algebra/total-owed` — **loaded, activated, and owing nothing.**
;;;
;;; `23_total_owed.ein` with Ann's arrow added, and the only difference between
;;; the two files is the line `(likes Ann Soup)`. There it is one outstanding
;;; obligation; here it is none.
;;;
;;; ── Why the negative case is the one that matters ─────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The coverage gate is satisfied by `23` alone: it activates the rule and the
;;; rule reports. What `23` cannot show is that the report ever **stops** — a
;;; `total-owed` whose guard was inverted, or whose `absent` scanned the wrong
;;; relation, would fire on this state too and `23` would still pass. Discharge
;;; is the guard (there is no second `∃b: G ∧ B` query to disagree with it), so
;;; the claim that the guard is right *is* the claim that this file owes
;;; nothing.
;;;
;;; That is P1c.1's pair, in the form the obligation duals need it: not only
;;; "the obligation is reported" but "it is not reported *here*", which is
;;; where a guard bug lives.
;;;
;;; ── What the expectation observes ─────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The model, which pins the state; the owe count — zero — is asserted in
;;; `ein-infer/tests/obligation_reports.rs`, because `:expect` speaks only of
;;; facts and an `open` conclusion is never one. See `23`'s note.

(import std.algebra :symbols (total-owed))

(relation is-a  T T         :why "{?1} is a {?2}")
(relation likes Person Food :why "{?1} likes {?2}")

(is-a Ann Person) (is-a Bob Person)
(is-a Soup Food)  (is-a Stew Food)

(total-owed likes is-a)

(likes Ann Soup)             ; the line `23` does not have
(likes Bob Stew)

(query
  :goal (likes ?who ?what)
  :no-hypothesis (is-a likes)
  :expect (model (likes Ann Soup) (likes Bob Stew)))
